    "turn_text": "I mean, look, I think that's unfair because they really tried to kill a lot of people. Like, come on, dude. In the 1990s, he said that if he's ever elected president, remember, he might be president soon, he would stage a military coup of his own on the very first day. I will say, if you're interested in an outsider out of the box thinking, it's not generally the person who just won power in a country that stages a coup, but I get where he's going with it. He would not be happy with the sort of powers normally given to a democratically elected president in Brazil. He instead wants absolute power as a dictator. So again, They claim that he's a threat to democracy. He claims he's a threat to democracy. He promises that he's a threat to democracy. But the Wall Street Journal can't actually be bothered to quote the guy himself. Now look, this is by far the biggest issue with Bolsonaro is that he apparently wants to, as is often the case around the world, ride into power through democracy and then shut the door after he's in and get rid of it and remove democracy as a future way that the people could get power back against. But there are other concerns with him as well. He is an incredibly, in their words, I'm a lib, so I think he's offensive and says things about identity politics. Okay, well look, they don't actually quote him there. They don't give you any evidence to decide whether they're being honest there. So why don't we read some of his quotes and see if he's just saying politically incorrect things. Here's a few quotes of many on homosexuality. He says, quote, if I see two men kissing in the street, I will beat them. If your child starts to become like that, a little gay, you take a whip and you change their behavior. He said he'd rather have a son die in an accident than turn out to be gay. And I guess it would save him the trouble of having to whip the kid. And he said, quote, liberal habits and working women have caused an increase in the number of gay people. For context, he said that to Ellen Page was a gay actress. So that's on homosexuality. So what does he think about women? He said to a female legislator, I would not rape you, you don't merit that. And then after people were frustrated with him for saying that the only reason he won't rape a woman is because she's not hot enough to deserve it. He went on Twitter and said that he had put her in her, quote, proper place. Now here's the thing, he is commonly called in Western media, Brazil's Trump or tropical Trump, a label that he doesn't seem to like, although we'll go into some of the more evidence on that. But I want to read some of his quotes and you'll sort of get how philosophically they're kind of similar. He called the United Nations International Human Rights Day, quote, the day of losers. So he didn't say losers and haters, to be fair, but he did say losers. He was worried about people from Haiti coming in and spreading disease, which is something Donald Trump has said. He called refugees from Syria the scum of the earth, which might actually be farther than what Donald Trump has said on them. Although to be fair, he does want to borrow them from actually coming here. And he wants to condemn them to dying in their own country. On race in 2011 during an appearance on a television program, he was asked by a famous Afro-Brazilian actress, Pretagil, what he would do if his son fell in love with a black woman. And he replied, I am not going to discuss promiscuity. He then added that he doesn't run that risk because his sons quote are very well raised telling the actress that they quote, don't live in the type of environment that you unfortunately do. Wow, that is something. Now, so issues with homosexuality, refugees, weird views on disease, race, women, very misogynistic, all of that. But like I said, he does reject the idea that he is Brazil's Trump. Although to be fair in 2016, he said, I like Trump's position. The only difference is that I'm richer. He's not politically correct, and that's why he's massacred by left wing media. So I like that the guy who wants the dictatorship in a military coup casually throws around the word massacre. That seems like a bad call. But again, to return to the Wall Street Journal, this is what he has actually said. And I would imagine that an organization with the history and the resources and the manpower that you have that on the odd opportunity, which does not often come where you decide to effectively endorse a foreign leader as the next president of that country, you could at the very least do a little bit of research and be minimally intellectually honest with your readership about what this person actually represents. But of course, that's not what you're interested in. You have a narrative to push. And by the way, that narrative barely has anything to do with Brazil. Do you honestly think that they are that invested in the future leadership of Brazil? I'm sure that they're fine with it and they don't mind a far right winger getting in there if he stops some left wing Brazilian from becoming president. But this is about the normalization, not of Jair Bolsonaro, but of leaders like him. This is for the far right leadership that's trying to move up in the ranks in Europe. This is for Donald Trump and others like him in the US as well. Because if you can get the American people to accept Jair Bolsonaro, a guy who says he wants a military coup, he wants to whip gays, and he will rape only the hottest women, then they will accept any sort of white nationalist racist a-hole in America as well. And I believe that that is the goal of that editorial in the Wall Street Journal. Okay, with that said, we are gonna take our first break. When we come back, Rokana Congressman is gonna be joining us. He is proposing a fairly all encompassing internet bill of rights, very exciting stuff that will break down after this. Not long ago, a data breach at Facebook exposed the personal data of tens of millions of users at the very least. And that's the sort of tech nightmare that has become so commonplace that, honestly, do you even remember that happening? So, look, considering that, is it time for some sort of internet bill of rights? And we are lucky enough to be joined on the damage court now by a congressman, Rokana, who is proposing exactly that. Rokana, welcome to the show. Great to be back on. Great to have you back on. And I'm very excited about this most recent proposal of yours. I mean, you're fresh off of a great victory with the Stop Bezos Act. So I'm excited to see what you can accomplish next. You are proposing an Internet",
