    "turn_text": "person that got away. The men laughing in the background. That's the Me Too movement. Also, the person that got away, I guess the expression is the one that got away. That doesn't seem too offensive. I think that you're allowed to say that. Maybe you think it's the girl who got away? That doesn't seem that bad. What was he actually thinking the expression was if he needed his self-sensor in that case? So look, the issue here is the constant mocking and bullying is coming from inside of the White House. If she actually wanted to do something about that, she has an opportunity literally every day. She has all these people on her phone, she can text them if she wants to. So while I like that you have had some sympathy for Melania, you are good progressives, you are filled with compassion for people, sometimes the people you feel this compassion for don't necessarily deserve it. And there are a lot of people that could use your empathy and your sympathy, save it for the people that actually deserve it. With that said, we're going to take a break. We come back. Voter suppression is coming up right before the election. We'll break it down for you. Welcome back to the damage for everybody. You know what? Let's take a second from our prepared stories and you're singing down there right now. Some good news for you, two bits of good news actually. The first is what you're seeing in right here. That's Washington State strikes down their death penalty. So five of their justices said, and this is the second bit of good news, that the death penalty is invalid specifically because it's imposed in an arbitrary and racially biased manner. which is even better than the first bit. So look, any state that gets rid of the death penalty, certainly a good thing, and it is a very slow process of getting more states to actually do that. Even states, by the way, that don't use the death penalty, still keep it on the books for one reason or another. But if it is going to be overturned, that is exactly why it needs to be overturned, because we know from the experience of many people, including innocent people who've been executed in that particular way, that it is arbitrary, it is racially biased. Certain types of people are far more likely to get the death penalty than others. And so good job Washington State in striking that down. Now it's interesting to note the last time that they actually used the death penalty in Washington State was back in 1994. So it still took decades to actually get rid of it. But interesting bit of trivia, in 1994 when they killed Charles Rodbin Campbell for two murders back in 1982, do you know how they did it? They hung the guy in 1994, our government hung someone. This is not 1894, this is a 1994. This is not Red Dead Redemption. This is literally what our government does to people. So, good job Washington in striking it down. Or if I can go back to my normal pessimism, perhaps temporarily striking it down because we now have an even more conservative Supreme Court. So we'll see if that gets appealed, what they'll actually think about it. Sorry, I don't want to be too pessimistic. And with that, why don't we move on to more bad news actually. Georgia has a very important governor's race going on right now, and you have two very different candidates running in it. So you have Stacey Abrams, who is a great progressive candidate. I believe she's the first African-American woman nominee for the governorship of Georgia in history. uh... she is running against brian camp that is george's secretary of state now if you're not familiar with this the secretary of state of the states is different than of the country the country they deal with a lot of things a lot of it is foreign policy in the states though a lot of it is election laws including voting laws who gets to vote how they get to vote That's Brian Kemp, who does that? He's now running an election, getting to set the rules for the election, and already it 