ian Kemp is considered a toss up according to a collection of many polls being done by real clear politics. So, as of right now, 1% could well be the deciding factor. There have been a number of races in the primary so far that's far closer than 1%. And when it comes to the governorship of an important state like Georgia, it is incredibly vital that everyone that wants to run actually gets to run. Now, right now, Many people are getting involved to put pressure on Brian Kemp and the Republicans in Georgia to do something about this. The ACLU is doing a good job. They have been on the case of Georgia for some time, actually. Back in August, they accused one of the state's counties of trying to make it harder for African Americans to vote. When the Randolph County Elections Board closed seven of its nine polling locations, that's a story that we covered at the time. And thankfully, with the pressure that the ACLU and other organizations and individuals put on them, they largely reversed that. But that does not mean that we are allowed to let down our guard. After the Supreme Court struck down key provisions of the Voting Rights Act a few years ago, it has been incredibly popular in southern states to do everything they can to wipe out people's ability to vote. And in races like this, especially historic races, like with Stacey Abrams potentially becoming the first female African American governor, it is incredibly important. So continue to put pressure on them. A fast little story. We know that Donald Trump and the Republicans have been trying to scare people over MS-13. They talk about MS-13 more than MS-13 talks about MS-13. And it apparently is having an effect, not just in stoking more xenophobia amongst Americans, but even in terms of how they spend their money. So one of the things that he has said is that you don't just need to worry about MS-13 in LA or near the border, these big cities, but in Long Island. He keeps coming back to it, Long Island, Long Island,